  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Co-ordinating and supporting the civil society response to HIV and AIDS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Cambodia, civil society organizations have played an important role in responding to the impact of the HIV/ AIDS epidemic, and have contributed to filling gaps within the government response. The HIV/ AIDS Coordinating Committee (HACC) is a membership organisation representing 120 NGOs (non-governmental organisations) and CBOs (community-based organisations). It acts as a forum for HIV/ AIDS information exchange, and plays an important role in co-ordinating the effort of its members in responding to HIV &#x2013; especially those which focus on Most At Risk Populations (MARPs).  It promotes partnership and collaboration among and between NGOs and government, advocates for policy change and to ensure respect for the rights of people to receive proper information and services, free from stigma and discrimination.  HACC also brings together NGOs working on HIV/ AIDS to campaign for coherent and co-ordinated prevention and care/ support for people living with HIV/ AIDS, and to help identify gaps in HIV services by co-ordinating evidence-based advocacy to government and relevant stakeholders on policy, planning, and monitoring progress.  Its activities support the full range of a comprehensive response to the epidemic: this includes networking, coordination, information sharing, awareness raising, representing NGOs to donors, national and international level advocacy for change and promoting the full involvement of civil society in determining policy, setting of national targets, developing plans and monitoring process in relation to HIV/ AIDS.  HACC makes a valuable contribution to Cambodia&#x2019;s nationwide response in the areas of prevention, impact mitigation, care, support and treatment.</narrative>
  </description>
